    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for Ghost Ride the WHIP hinges on capitalizing on high-ceiling matchups in favorable hitting environments, with **Seiya Suzuki** (69.51) and **Corbin Carroll** (69.14) emerging as our top-tier plays. **Seiya Suzuki** draws a juicy matchup against Andrew Abbott, where a 4.42 xERA and a positive platoon edge, combined with an 87% stolen base success rate against the pitcher\u2019s delivery, make him an elite start. Similarly, **Corbin Carroll** remains a core anchor; despite a modest -6% SP skill adjustment for his matchup against Bubba Chandler, his position in the three-hole and a robust +6% platoon advantage justify his high ranking. **James Wood** also earns a strong recommendation at 69.03, as he continues to display elite xwOBA (0.377) and benefits significantly from a leadoff assignment against Triston Bradley.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the bottom of the order and our utility spot. **Josh Naylor** (63.24) earns the start over **Jose Caballero** (41.62) because, while we prioritize stolen base threats, the massive gulf in base-projection and the overwhelming difficulty of the matchup against Jacob deGrom made **Jose Caballero** an easy cut. **Zach McKinstry** (60.52) narrowly holds his spot in the lineup, buoyed by a +12% SP skill boost and wind assistance, allowing him to outperform bench options like **Alex Freeland**, who fell below our 40.0 \"Below Floor\" threshold. We are monitoring **Tyler Soderstrom** closely; his 43.82 score is bolstered by wind, but his -19% SP skill penalty against Jes\u00fas Luzardo makes him a high-risk play that could be pulled if game caps become a concern later in the week.\n\nInvestors should note the total loss of **Mickey Moniak**, whose game has already been flagged for a rainout. With **Luis Campusano** also riding the pine due to a daunting matchup against Logan Webb and lingering lineup uncertainty, we are maintaining a disciplined approach to our daily game caps. **Austin Riley** (46.64) and **Dalton Rushing** (49.29) remain marginal starts for the day; while we aren't expecting massive production, their lineup placement and platoon status provide enough stability to avoid benching them in favor of the lower-floor options currently available on our reserves, such as **Brice Turang**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Max Muncy**, **Carson Williams**, or **Kyle Karros**.",
